Rewritten 2026-07-05 from the repo review; pruned the same day after
the first work wave landed. The 2018 list is fully absorbed by the
bartcore rewrite (see docs/design/); "warn on possible factors" is
retired as obsolete. DONE and removed from this list: the five
verified review bugs (gp zero weights, rbart_vi prior lookup by name,
silent weights recycling, chi-k Inf guard, dead-code/message cleanups;
bart2-vs-rbart_vi k handling resolved as deliberate - rbart forwards
through do.call so it must store the evaluated value), release
metadata (AC_INIT, SystemRequirements: C++20, .win header versions,
Description text), CI triggers for the bartcore branch +
workflow_dispatch, man-page examples for every headline feature,
logistic k/binaryOffset doc fixes, MIA and gp references, README
refresh with badge and package-authors note, and seven new coverage
test files (logistic+offset, DART x categorical x MIA, boundary
inputs, grouped weighted/binary halves + refusal, mutate-then-
serialize bitwise for all three leaf types, bridge errors, plot
smoke).

release
  push dbarts 1.0-0 (branch bartcore) and stan4bart 0.0-13; submit both
    to CRAN together
  stan4bart follow-up: adopt categorical splits (pins factors =
    "indicators"); lockstep with the dbarts release
  revdepcheck before submission: dbarts has CRAN reverse dependencies
    (stan4bart, bartCause, ...) and 1.0-0 deletes an ABI header - run
    revdepcheck::revdep_check(), coordinate the stan4bart lockstep
    upload, and write cran-comments.md documenting the break and the
    revdep status (no cran-comments.md exists)
  Windows-arm64 runs scalar kernels: the .win config headers never
    define COMPILER_SUPPORTS_NEON and Makevars.win's ARCH check has no
    aarch64 branch; consider a NEON branch
  verified clean by the build review: state mask serialization is
    byte-shifted (endian-safe); .Call registration complete; dbarts.h
    is C89-clean and matches the bridge; tarball free of build junk;
    sublibrary Makefiles own the SIMD source lists cross-platform

usability
  thread cancellation (2018 carryover): DONE. run() now polls a
    cooperative cancel predicate between sweeps (chain.hpp returns early;
    order-preserving so equivalence stays bitwise identical - all 18
    scenarios). Single-chain polls R_CheckUserInterrupt inline (throttled
    ~100ms via R_ToplevelExec so the longjmp is caught and threads can be
    joined); multi-chain has the main thread poll and set an atomic flag the
    workers read, with SIGINT blocked in workers (POSIX) so only the main
    thread runs R's handler. The bridge turns a cancelled run into an
    Rf_error after every worker is joined. Verified: pty Ctrl-C interrupts
    both paths in ~0.3-0.5s with R healthy after; tests/cpp
    testRunCancellation gates the engine mechanism; ASAN+UBSAN and TSAN
    (zero races) clean. Interactive Ctrl-C can't be exercised in tinytest
    (non-tty Rscript never sets the interrupt flag), so the pty check stays
    out of CI - the C++ test covers the mechanism.
  weights + binary refusal fires at sampler creation in the bridge
    (good message, wrong layer): DONE, and extended. The refusal now lives
    in the R layer (dbarts.R, bridge backstops direct-API consumers) and is
    split by family: probit is refused (no tractable weighted latent form),
    logistic accepts positive-integer weights as observation counts (the
    Polya-Gamma latent PG(w, psi) is a sum of w PG(1, psi) draws), gaussian
    is unrestricted. See docs/design/weighted-logistic.md. Deferred there:
    arbitrary real logistic weights (needs a general real-shape PG sampler;
    the survey/IPW use case wants it but is a pseudo-likelihood trap).
  rbart_vi's sparse refusal doesn't say why or point at dbarts/bart2,
    which do support sparse. DONE: the message now names them.
  bart/bart2 up-front numeric argument validation: MOSTLY ALREADY DONE.
    power/base validate through the dbartsCGMPrior S4 validity, k through
    normal(), and the counts through dbartsControl's validity - so bad
    values report an informative message (wrapped in the standard S4
    "invalid class ... object:" prefix), not an opaque parsePriors error.
    The one genuine gap was n.thin = 0, which passed validity (non-
    negative) and then divided by zero downstream (n.burn %/% n.thin ->
    NA -> "missing value where TRUE/FALSE needed"); n.thin is now required
    positive. Residual nit (deferred, package-wide cosmetic): the S4
    validity prefix leaks internal class names to users; stripping it
    consistently is a separate pass, not a per-constructor patch.

api surface
  xbart cannot reach DART or split.probs: DONE. xbart now takes
    split.probs (fixed, non-uniform variable weights) and dart (TRUE or a
    dart() spec) like bart2, resolved against the fold data and swept over
    the power/base grid. A DART sampler's sampled Dirichlet state persists
    across the warm-cell setModel path (chain.hpp skips the split-prob
    install when useDart), and the default uniform cgm is bit-identical, so
    the reproducibility snapshots are unchanged.
  dbarts() has no seed argument: DONE. dbarts() now takes seed, a
    convenience mirror of dbartsControl(rngSeed = ) as bart2/xbart expose;
    an explicit seed overrides the control's, NA leaves it untouched.
  credible-interval API: DONE (Vincent confirmed the shape 2026-07-05).
    fitted() and predict() take a scalar ci.level; when set they return a
    per-observation matrix of est/ci.lower/ci.upper instead of the posterior
    mean. The interval KIND rides the existing type axis rather than a new
    flag: "ev" is a credible interval for the mean (probability for binary),
    "ppd" a wider prediction interval that adds residual noise, "bart" the
    latent scale - matching base R's confidence/prediction split and
    rstanarm's epred/predict split (see the ecosystem survey in the session
    notes; bartMachine splits it into two functions instead). A standalone
    summary() method is still open but now trivial (it would just call this).
    residuals link scale: DONE.
    residuals.bart/.rbart now take a type argument that flows to fitted
    (sample pinned to "train"), so type = "bart" gives link-scale
    residuals; default "ev" is unchanged. Fixed alongside: binary bart()
    fits did not store y, so residuals() returned numeric(0) on them - the
    binary result branch now keeps y like the gaussian one (rbart already
    did).
  plotTree fit-level dispatch: DONE. plotTree is now an exported S3
    generic with methods for bart, rbart, and dbartsSampler, so a kept
    fit plots a tree directly instead of reaching into $fit$plotTree.
    plotTree.rbart mirrors extract's chain indirection (one in-core
    multi-chain sampler selects the chain through its own chainNum; the
    R-loop path keeps one sampler per chain). man/plotTree.Rd added.

performance (measure before acting. The ORDER-PRESERVING items landed
as we went, bitwise identical; the ORDER-CHANGING batch landed
2026-07-06 as the deliberate one-time bitwise drop - equivalence and
speed baselines re-recorded, NEWS notes the trade. The RNG stream and
realized accept/reject decisions survived the reorder, so the
tolerance-based test snapshots did not need regeneration; the
equivalence gate now compares statistically against pre-drop anchors,
reporting max |z| = 0.00 on all 18 scenarios. Cumulative vs the classic
engine at the drop: run scenarios 25-30% less time per iteration.)
  run-loop pass fusion, ORDER-PRESERVING: DONE. residual build (was
    memcpy+subtract+add) fused to one (y - total) + old pass, fit swap
    (was subtract+add) fused to one pass; restrict-qualified locals,
    left-to-right order preserved so bitwise identical (equivalence
    reports identical draws on all 17 scenarios). Same-climate A/B: run
    scenarios 0.90-0.93x (8-10% faster, 9.7% at n=10000 - the win scales
    with n). The two setPredictor microbenchmarks moved +7/+13% but share
    NO code with the fusion (it is inline in run()), so that is
    code-layout jitter on 0.15ms measurements, not a real regression.
  draw into the fit slab, ORDER-PRESERVING: DONE (d688b19). The draw now
    writes each tree's new fits straight into its treeFits_ slab; the
    residual pass also strips the tree's old fits from totalFits so the
    slab can be overwritten in place, and the running total is restored by
    adding the slab back. The trailing memcpy and the currFits_ scratch
    buffer are gone: two O(n) passes per tree instead of three. totalFits
    stays (total - oldFits) + newFits in order, bitwise identical
    (equivalence identical on all 17); A/B 3-6% faster on the p=10 run
    scenarios (embedded-offset's +6.8% flag was within its ~13% same-binary
    noise band, verified by a noise-floor run).
  running residual, ORDER-CHANGING (the deliberate end-of-work bitwise
    drop): DONE. treeY_ carries y - totalFits across the sweep: one
    fused pass per tree retires the previous tree's new fits and admits
    this tree's old ones (in place of three totalFits-maintenance
    passes), the draw overwrites the slab, and totalFits_ is rebuilt
    once per sweep - retiring the last tree's fits in the same pass -
    for the latent/sigma/record consumers. The RNG stream and
    accept/reject decisions are unchanged in practice (values move at
    rounding scale only), so the tolerance-based test snapshots survived
    untouched; the one bitwise casualty is that a zero-weight row's own
    reported fit now depends on its response at rounding scale (y enters
    and leaves through the rebuild), and test-zero-weights.R pins the
    sharper form (retained rows bitwise, perturbed rows to 1e-12).
    Same-climate A/B: run scenarios 0.85-0.95 (15% faster at n=10000
    where the O(n) passes dominate, 5-6% at n=1000), nothing regressed.
  variable availability in one ancestor walk, ORDER-PRESERVING: DONE
    (c3f5915). numVariablesAvailable re-walked the ancestors once per
    predictor, O(p x depth) at every node scored in a move; replaced by
    Tree::hasAnyAvailableVariable (early-exit boolean for growthProbability
    and the birth-node predicates) and Tree::collectAvailableVariables (one
    walk narrowing every ordinal interval and inline-categorical mask,
    O(p + depth); pooled >63-level columns fall back to the per-column
    walk). splitVariableLogProbability/drawSplitVariable read the cached
    array. Nested bounds make the extremum equal the nearest-wins bound, so
    counts/sums/i-th draw are bitwise identical (equivalence identical on all
    17); same-climate A/B 8-10% faster on the p=10 run scenarios (1.2% at
    n=10000 where O(n) passes dominate). The order-changing half is DONE
    with the running-residual batch: change/swap now score
    treeLogProbability from the modified node instead of the root - prior
    terms outside the subtree depend only on rules above it, so they are
    identical on both sides of the ratio and cancel (availability walks
    ancestors only; verified in tree.hpp collectAvailableVariables).
  skip setNodeAverages under linear leaves, ORDER-PRESERVING: DONE
    (44a9383). The constant-leaf per-node means are pure waste for linear
    (vector) leaves, which accumulate their own statistics and never read
    node.average; guarded with if constexpr (!hasVectorParams) so it still
    runs for the constant leaf AND for gp (whose over-cap nodes delegate to
    the constant leaf - a first guard that skipped gp too shifted the gp
    component-test values and was caught before commit). Bitwise identical
    (equivalence all 17 identical); same-climate A/B on a q=2 linear run
    5.13s -> 4.31s (16% faster, tight distributions). STILL OPEN and NOT
    pursued: caching the crossproduct across the linear-leaf score->draw
    gap - the marginal and draw run on different node sets (the draw covers
    all bottom nodes; move marginals only the touched branch), so a true
    reuse needs a persistent per-node stats cache, which the design
    deliberately avoids ("move rollbacks need no cache invalidation",
    model.hpp:162-165). Not the free win the note implied.
  rbart_vi custom-prior R loop pays a full bridge round trip per
    posterior sample (rbart.R: setOffset + run(0L, 1L), ~10 fresh
    SEXPs per call in bartcore_run); built-in priors already bypass
    via in-core. Profile before touching; only that path pays.
  test-fit routing is serial over test rows (chain.hpp findBottom per
    row per tree); rows are independent chains so parallelizing is
    order-preserving - only matters when numTest ~ n.
  x86 SIMD is silently scalar: the SSE2/AVX bodies of
    misc_addVectorsInPlace, misc_subtractVectorsInPlace, and
    misc_setVectorToConstant contain scalar loops with the real
    intrinsics commented out (linearAlgebra_sse2.c:173-215 and
    siblings); dispatch tables and cpuid detection all wired; NEON
    arms live. Likely LOW impact - the scalar bodies autovectorize at
    -O2 on modern compilers - so either restore the intrinsics after
    measuring on x86 or simplify away the dead dispatch weight.
    misc_setIndexedVectorToConstant never entered the dispatch table
    at all. The SSR kernel has no SIMD leaf, unlike its mean/variance
    neighbors.
  threading: chains only (sampler.hpp std::thread); within-chain
    tree-level parallelism is structurally blocked by backfitting, and
    the observation-level htm machinery is dormant by design (all call
    sites pass a null manager - the serial unrolled accumulators are the
    fast path). Threaded within-tree reductions were ASSESSED AND
    DEFERRED with the order-changing batch: backfitting serializes
    trees, so parallelism lives inside each O(n) pass, costing ~3
    barrier syncs x numTrees per sweep (~300 at the defaults) against
    2.1 ms/iteration at n = 10^4 - overhead on the order of the win.
    Measured headroom exists only at n >= 10^5, where the sweep goes
    DRAM-bound (31 ms/iteration at n = 10^5, 14.6x the n = 10^4 time)
    and extra cores add bandwidth. Wiring it also makes results depend
    on n.threads (per-thread-count determinism at best), a new
    reproducibility surface. Revisit if single-chain n ~ 10^5 workloads
    materialize; the htm machinery and this analysis are the starting
    point. Verified clean: the hot loop has no allocation churn
    (scratch reused throughout).

internals (all but the first landed 2026-07-06 as refactor-only
commits: the equivalence gate reported identical draws on all 18
scenarios afterward, suite 2477/0, speed flat)
  chain.hpp opens the scalar/vector/function three-way if constexpr in
    ~12 methods (45 sites); hoisting per-shape helpers would make a
    fourth leaf shape touch one place instead of twelve. Large; the
    equivalence gate arbitrates. DECISION PENDING with Vincent - the
    proposed shape is ~8 named per-operation Chain helpers (flatten,
    prior draw, state channels, fits rebuild, storage reset, test
    routing), each holding one three-way, rather than a policy object
    that would need friend access to half of Chain.
  bridge: DONE. createHolder and bartcore_createFromHandle share
    parseSamplerSpecification; the binary-weights mutation refusal is
    one helper; bartcore_run roots each result column in its protected
    container as allocated (no hand-counted UNPROTECT); getTrees is
    validate -> gatherTrees -> emitTreeDataFrame.
  bart/bart2/rbart_vi prior construction: DONE (buildSamplerPriors,
    with nodeK keeping the evaluated/unevaluated asymmetry documented
    at the call sites).
  createClassicSampler rename + designation-check dedup: DONE
    (createConstantLeafSampler; leafCovariateDesignationIsValid).
  validateArgumentsInEnvironment messages: DONE (callers pass their
    display name, so xbart errors no longer claim to be dbarts's).
  partitionFlatIndices hardening: DONE (both category lookups clamp,
    identity for valid codes).
  Makevars.win ARFLAGS: DONE (mirrors Makevars.in's -crs fallback).

tests (remaining gaps from the coverage review)
  the equivalence gate now covers logistic, categorical, MIA, adaptive
    DART, linear/gp leaves, sparse, and exact-zero weights (eight added
    scenarios driven through the sampler API, baseline re-recorded at
    19c499a); grouped rbart_vi fits stay out - their multi-chain result
    shape does not fit the single-fit summary, so add a scenario only if
    a grouped refactor is planned.
  zero-weight fits for the constant and linear leaves: DONE
    (test-zero-weights.R). Oracle: perturbing a zero-weight row's response
    leaves the fit bitwise unchanged (it contributes nothing to any leaf's
    sufficient statistics), for both constant and linear leaves. The
    perturbation is kept inside the response range - extremes held by
    non-zero-weight rows - because the gaussian transform is range-based
    (fitMin/fitMax), so an out-of-range change legitimately shifts the fit.
  post-mutation assertions are near-tautologies (is.finite only) in
    the older linear/gp mutation tests; the new mutate-then-serialize
    file covers the bitwise side, but comparing against a
    from-scratch sampler after mutation would strengthen the rest.
    (Note: setPredictor is NOT bitwise-equal to a fresh build even before
    any run - it re-quantizes differently - so a from-scratch oracle must
    be statistical, not identical; see the MIA setPredictor test.)
  MIA setPredictor that moves NA patterns: DONE (test-data-missing.R). On
    an incorporate sampler, setPredictor installs a replacement column whose
    missingness carries the response signal; the refit recovers it (it was
    unusable before), fits stay finite, and the installed column carries the
    moved NA rows.

docs
  prose-level man-page pass before release: DONE. Audited every man page
    against current behavior; fixed the stale logistic-weights claim
    (dbarts/bart), two return-class typos (dbartControl/dbartData),
    the undocumented setControl/setModel restrictions and binary-weight
    policy in dbartsSampler-class, and the rbart_vi no-family/no-sparse
    caveat. Remaining prose verified clean (no classic-engine or
    R_C_interface references anywhere).
  vignettes predate the rewrite: working_with_saved_trees.Rmd seed DONE.
    seed = 2 no longer generated a deep tree under the new engine (5 nodes,
    depth 3); changed to seed = 56 (13 nodes, 4 split variables, richer
    interaction matrix), verified reproducible. All tree output is in live
    knitr chunks and the prose is generic, so no other changes were needed.
    The mixture vignette (gibbs_sampler_mixture_model.Rmd) was verified to
    still hold: deterministic from set.seed(2793), 11.7% misclassification,
    p recovered to 0.499 vs 0.5, tail-regression claim confirmed - no change
    needed. STILL OPEN (optional polish): neither vignette mentions family,
    dart, sparse, missing, or the new leaf models; the saved-trees traversal
    code assumes constant leaves (linear/gp change the getTrees columns).
  NEWS could consolidate an upgrade-path paragraph (the breaking
    changes are documented but scattered across bullets).

repo modernization (existing infra: r-lib R-CMD-check matrix now
running on the bartcore branch, .lintr, air.toml, Authors@R modern)
  keep the CI action pins current: actions/checkout bumped v4 -> v7 to
    clear the Node 20 deprecation warning (r-lib/actions stays @v2, the
    latest major, so it was never flagged); recheck on each push and bump
    again when GitHub deprecates a runtime.
  sanitizer coverage: .github/workflows/sanitizers.yaml runs the tinytest
    suite on the r-hub clang-asan container (ASAN+UBSAN instrumented R),
    widening past the local tests/cpp check. The engine is already clean
    under a native ASAN+UBSAN build of tests/cpp (all 48 component tests,
    zero findings); the CI job adds the bridge, the support libraries, and
    the full scenario set on x86. Follow-ups: a gcc-asan/valgrind variant,
    and gating it on pull_request once it has proven stable on the branch.
  run tests/cpp in CI: DONE (.github/workflows/cpp-tests.yaml). Builds the
    package in place and runs the C++ component tests on macos-latest
    (arm64) - matching where the bitwise baselines are recorded, so the
    tight-tolerance/determinism checks do not drift on last-ulp reduction
    order; the test binary exits non-zero on failure so the job gates. On
    push to bartcore + PR + workflow_dispatch. STILL OPEN: the equivalence
    gate could ride CI too but needs its statistical (z-score) mode wired
    cross-platform, since its bitwise baselines are ARM-recorded.
  coverage measurement: covr supports tinytest; wiring it to codecov
    (badge + CI job) turns the tests section above from one-time
    review findings into a tracked number.
  pkgdown site + GitHub Pages workflow: renders the man pages, NEWS,
    and both vignettes into a browsable home for the 1.0-0 feature
    set; near-zero maintenance once configured.
  enforce .lintr/air in CI or drop them: both configs exist but
    nothing runs them, so they silently rot.
  optional, larger, taste-dependent: roxygen2 migration (hand-written
    Rd is complete and codoc-clean today; migration buys inline
    doc-next-to-code at the cost of a huge one-time diff);
    NEWS.md instead of inst/NEWS.Rd (pkgdown renders it natively).

